WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.200
Instructor: So, there's one more concept

00:01.200 --> 00:02.160
we need to get out of the way

00:02.160 --> 00:04.410
before we can talk about creating branches

00:04.410 --> 00:06.270
which is this thing right here.

00:06.270 --> 00:07.103
Head.

00:07.103 --> 00:08.880
You've probably seen this before

00:08.880 --> 00:12.960
when we type git log, for example, every commit shows up.

00:12.960 --> 00:14.430
Hopefully that's review.

00:14.430 --> 00:17.850
And in our case, we see at the most recent commit

00:17.850 --> 00:21.810
the top one, this thing, head, arrow, master.

00:21.810 --> 00:23.580
So, what exactly is that thing?

00:23.580 --> 00:26.430
Head in all caps, what does it mean?

00:26.430 --> 00:28.920
Well, it's just a term in Git.

00:28.920 --> 00:31.020
Specifically it is a pointer

00:31.020 --> 00:34.590
that refers to our current location in the repository

00:34.590 --> 00:37.650
and it points to a particular branch reference.

00:37.650 --> 00:39.360
So, there's some terminology in there.

00:39.360 --> 00:41.190
Branch reference we have not discussed.

00:41.190 --> 00:43.110
So, I'm gonna break it down here.

00:43.110 --> 00:48.090
I like to think of branches as bookmarks in a book.

00:48.090 --> 00:50.370
We can have a whole bunch of different bookmarks

00:50.370 --> 00:52.380
different places in a book.

00:52.380 --> 00:56.220
So, let's say this is where I left off in the book

00:56.220 --> 00:58.200
and this is where I'm sharing it with my friend.

00:58.200 --> 00:59.820
This is where Fitz left off

00:59.820 --> 01:02.310
and this is where Ethel left off in the book.

01:02.310 --> 01:03.633
Three different spots.

01:04.500 --> 01:06.420
At any given point in time

01:06.420 --> 01:09.990
only one of those spots can be open, right?

01:09.990 --> 01:12.420
We can't have the book open to multiple pages.

01:12.420 --> 01:14.580
So, only one is active,

01:14.580 --> 01:17.721
only one is being read or being viewed.

01:17.721 --> 01:20.460
It's actually open to that bookmark.

01:20.460 --> 01:22.800
So, that is what HEAD refers to.

01:22.800 --> 01:26.340
HEAD is our current location that we are viewing

01:26.340 --> 01:27.750
or that we are checking out

01:27.750 --> 01:29.940
is a common term you'll hear.

01:29.940 --> 01:32.670
So, in our case, we haven't done anything with HEAD,

01:32.670 --> 01:34.050
we haven't made any branches.

01:34.050 --> 01:37.740
So, HEAD always is referring to our master branch.

01:37.740 --> 01:39.840
The last commit on our master branch.

01:39.840 --> 01:41.010
And that's what we see here.

01:41.010 --> 01:42.690
Everything's on our master branch.

01:42.690 --> 01:47.220
HEAD refers to master, that's what this arrow means.

01:47.220 --> 01:49.140
HEAD pointing to the master branch.

01:49.140 --> 01:51.840
And this is all master branch.

01:51.840 --> 01:53.730
This is what we call the tip of the branch.

01:53.730 --> 01:55.950
It's the last commit, the most recent one.

01:55.950 --> 01:59.790
So, HEAD refers to the branch pointer.

01:59.790 --> 02:01.560
I know it's crazy terminology.

02:01.560 --> 02:03.570
Then if I had more branches,

02:03.570 --> 02:05.640
if I switch to a different branch,

02:05.640 --> 02:07.860
like I switched to Ethel's branch,

02:07.860 --> 02:10.680
HEAD points to the tip of Ethel's branch,

02:10.680 --> 02:13.800
or I take a look at Fitz branch, I've switched there.

02:13.800 --> 02:16.590
This is what I have open in our massive book.

02:16.590 --> 02:20.370
This is the thing I currently have active in my repository.

02:20.370 --> 02:21.720
HEAD points there.

02:21.720 --> 02:25.050
I believe the term originates from a play head,

02:25.050 --> 02:28.530
I think, is what it's called on an old tape machine

02:28.530 --> 02:29.520
or tape recorder.

02:29.520 --> 02:31.170
I don't know, I'm out of my depth here

02:31.170 --> 02:34.620
but it had some analog meaning in the real world.

02:34.620 --> 02:37.470
So, one more diagram to make this clear.

02:37.470 --> 02:38.940
Probably won't make it clear just yet

02:38.940 --> 02:40.440
but more practice will.

02:40.440 --> 02:41.730
I want to reiterate.

02:41.730 --> 02:42.780
HEAD is a pointer.

02:42.780 --> 02:47.010
It's a reference to a branch pointer.

02:47.010 --> 02:51.090
And a branch pointer is where a branch currently is.

02:51.090 --> 02:53.160
It's like that bookmark in a book.

02:53.160 --> 02:54.630
We can have a whole bunch of branches

02:54.630 --> 02:57.660
and each one has a branch reference

02:57.660 --> 03:00.180
that just refers to where that branch is.

03:00.180 --> 03:02.010
So, let me demonstrate this.

03:02.010 --> 03:03.270
We started on our master branch.

03:03.270 --> 03:04.170
That's what we are on now,

03:04.170 --> 03:05.460
and every project we've done,

03:05.460 --> 03:06.630
we're on master.

03:06.630 --> 03:09.030
HEAD points to master.

03:09.030 --> 03:09.863
I do some more work.

03:09.863 --> 03:10.696
I make a commit.

03:10.696 --> 03:12.360
I'm still on master.

03:12.360 --> 03:16.090
So, this branch pointer updates to my new commit

03:16.950 --> 03:18.060
and HEAD stays the same.

03:18.060 --> 03:19.860
It's still master

03:19.860 --> 03:22.980
but then I decide I'm going to try something experimental.

03:22.980 --> 03:24.690
I'm going to make a new branch.

03:24.690 --> 03:27.510
So, I want to try adding a dark mode to my website.

03:27.510 --> 03:28.800
So, I make a new branch.

03:28.800 --> 03:30.690
We'll learn how to do that soon.

03:30.690 --> 03:34.380
Dark mode still refers to the same commit as master.

03:34.380 --> 03:36.570
They are at the same point, right?

03:36.570 --> 03:39.900
They are two branch references pointing at the same thing

03:39.900 --> 03:41.850
and HEAD points to dark mode

03:41.850 --> 03:45.360
which means I'm on the dark mode branch.

03:45.360 --> 03:46.860
So, now if I make a new commit,

03:46.860 --> 03:50.100
I do some new work, things diverge.

03:50.100 --> 03:53.820
Master stays where it was, it's pointing to this commit.

03:53.820 --> 03:57.150
But dark mode now has a new commit it refers to

03:57.150 --> 03:59.520
and HEAD still points to dark mode.

03:59.520 --> 04:02.610
So, if I do new work, I'm on dark mode.

04:02.610 --> 04:04.563
That's the branch I currently am on.

04:05.490 --> 04:08.370
So, that new commit is on dark mode.

04:08.370 --> 04:10.710
This dark mode pointer updates

04:10.710 --> 04:12.990
to this commit instead of that one.

04:12.990 --> 04:15.240
And then I can switch back, at any point,

04:15.240 --> 04:16.980
I could go back to master,

04:16.980 --> 04:19.650
remember, I think of it as a bookmark in a book.

04:19.650 --> 04:21.120
So, we still have that bookmark.

04:21.120 --> 04:22.920
That's what this reference is.

04:22.920 --> 04:24.930
And I can open to that page

04:24.930 --> 04:27.300
by switching over to this branch.

04:27.300 --> 04:30.060
HEAD is pointing to master

04:30.060 --> 04:33.480
and I could go back to dark mode, go back to master.

04:33.480 --> 04:35.850
HEAD is just the thing that is currently open

04:35.850 --> 04:39.750
that I'm currently checking out in my repository.

04:39.750 --> 04:42.300
So, we can have hundreds of these different branches.

04:42.300 --> 04:44.010
Each branch has a branch reference

04:44.010 --> 04:45.720
pointing to where we left off.

04:45.720 --> 04:47.430
That's really the key takeaway here.

04:47.430 --> 04:49.800
A branch is just a reference to some commit.

04:49.800 --> 04:51.750
And as we make more commits,

04:51.750 --> 04:53.460
even if we're just on the master branch.

04:53.460 --> 04:55.050
I make a new commit,

04:55.050 --> 04:58.500
that master pointer now reflects that change.

04:58.500 --> 05:00.750
It updates to point to that new commit.

05:00.750 --> 05:04.680
So, over here, originally when this was the only commit,

05:04.680 --> 05:06.630
master was pointing to this commit

05:06.630 --> 05:08.790
and then to this commit, and then to this commit.

05:08.790 --> 05:11.700
And now it's all the way up to this commit here.

05:11.700 --> 05:12.930
They're all on the master branch.

05:12.930 --> 05:15.240
This is the tip of the master branch here.

05:15.240 --> 05:17.970
And then at any point we can create different branches

05:17.970 --> 05:20.100
and splinter off into different directions.

05:20.100 --> 05:22.140
We can have all these different branch pointers

05:22.140 --> 05:23.490
referring to different commits

05:23.490 --> 05:27.870
and then switch between them by moving HEAD.

05:27.870 --> 05:30.480
All right, so conceptually, it's kind of a dense video.

05:30.480 --> 05:31.313
I know.

05:31.313 --> 05:32.910
I think it'll make a lot more sense

05:32.910 --> 05:35.730
when I show you how to do this with the relevant commands

05:35.730 --> 05:36.993
which is coming up next.
